---
title: Archiving
order: 3
description: Compression and archiving — tar, gzip, bzip2, xz, zstd, zip, 7z
tags: [files, tar, gzip, bzip2, xz, zstd, zip, 7z, compression, archive]
---

Tools for creating archives and compressing files. tar handles archiving (bundling files), while gzip/bzip2/xz/zstd handle compression. zip and 7z combine both.

@text
tar — tape archive. Bundle files into a single archive, optionally compressed.

@code bash
# Create a tar archive
tar cf archive.tar file1 file2 dir/

# Create with verbose output
tar cvf archive.tar dir/

# Create gzip-compressed archive (.tar.gz or .tgz)
tar czf archive.tar.gz dir/

# Create bzip2-compressed archive (.tar.bz2)
tar cjf archive.tar.bz2 dir/

# Create xz-compressed archive (.tar.xz) — best ratio, slower
tar cJf archive.tar.xz dir/

# Create zstd-compressed archive (.tar.zst) — fast with good ratio
tar --zstd -cf archive.tar.zst dir/

# Extract an archive (auto-detects compression)
tar xf archive.tar.gz

# Extract to a specific directory
tar xf archive.tar.gz -C /dest/dir/

# Extract with verbose output
tar xvf archive.tar.gz

# List contents without extracting
tar tf archive.tar.gz

# Extract specific files
tar xf archive.tar.gz path/to/file.txt

# Exclude patterns when creating
tar czf archive.tar.gz --exclude='*.log' --exclude='.git' dir/

# Exclude from file
tar czf archive.tar.gz --exclude-from=exclude.txt dir/

# Append files to existing tar (uncompressed only)
tar rf archive.tar newfile.txt

# Preserve permissions and ownership
tar czpf archive.tar.gz dir/

# Create archive with absolute paths stripped (default behavior)
tar czf archive.tar.gz -C /path/to parent_dir/

# Incremental backup with tar
tar czf backup-$(date +%F).tar.gz --newer-mtime='2024-01-01' dir/

@text
Individual compression tools — compress/decompress single files or streams.

@code bash
# gzip — fast, ubiquitous, moderate ratio
gzip file.txt                    # compresses to file.txt.gz, removes original
gzip -k file.txt                 # keep original
gzip -d file.txt.gz              # decompress (or: gunzip file.txt.gz)
gzip -l file.txt.gz              # show compression info
gzip -1 file.txt                 # fastest compression
gzip -9 file.txt                 # best compression
zcat file.txt.gz                 # view compressed file without extracting

# bzip2 — slower, better ratio than gzip
bzip2 file.txt                   # compresses to file.txt.bz2
bzip2 -k file.txt                # keep original
bzip2 -d file.txt.bz2            # decompress (or: bunzip2)
bzcat file.txt.bz2               # view without extracting

# xz — best ratio, slowest
xz file.txt                      # compresses to file.txt.xz
xz -k file.txt                   # keep original
xz -d file.txt.xz                # decompress (or: unxz)
xz -T0 file.txt                  # use all CPU cores
xz -9 file.txt                   # maximum compression
xzcat file.txt.xz                # view without extracting

# zstd — excellent ratio with fast speed (best general-purpose)
zstd file.txt                    # compresses to file.txt.zst
zstd -k file.txt                 # keep original (default behavior)
zstd -d file.txt.zst             # decompress (or: unzstd)
zstd -T0 file.txt                # use all CPU cores
zstd -19 file.txt                # maximum compression
zstd --rm file.txt               # remove original after compressing
zstdcat file.txt.zst             # view without extracting

@text
zip and unzip — cross-platform archive format.

@code bash
# Create a zip archive
zip archive.zip file1 file2

# Zip a directory recursively
zip -r archive.zip dir/

# Add password protection
zip -e -r archive.zip dir/

# Exclude patterns
zip -r archive.zip dir/ -x "*.log" "*.tmp"

# Update existing zip (add new/changed files)
zip -u archive.zip newfile.txt

# List contents
unzip -l archive.zip

# Extract
unzip archive.zip

# Extract to specific directory
unzip archive.zip -d /dest/dir/

# Extract specific files
unzip archive.zip path/to/file.txt

# Test archive integrity
unzip -t archive.zip

# Overwrite without prompting
unzip -o archive.zip

@text
7z — high compression ratio, many format support.

@code bash
# Install p7zip
sudo pacman -S p7zip

# Create 7z archive
7z a archive.7z dir/

# Create with maximum compression
7z a -mx=9 archive.7z dir/

# Create with password and encryption
7z a -p -mhe=on archive.7z dir/

# Extract
7z x archive.7z

# Extract to directory
7z x archive.7z -o/dest/dir/

# List contents
7z l archive.7z

# Test integrity
7z t archive.7z

# Extract specific files
7z x archive.7z file.txt

# Create tar.7z (tar first, then 7z compress)
tar cf - dir/ | 7z a -si archive.tar.7z

@text
Compression comparison (typical text file, higher = better):

  Tool     Speed      Ratio     Notes
  gzip     Fast       Moderate  Universally available
  bzip2    Slow       Good      Largely superseded by xz/zstd
  xz       Slowest    Best      Great for distribution archives
  zstd     Fastest    Good      Best speed/ratio tradeoff
  7z       Slow       Best      Best for Windows interop
